A||||8||Jncomutt||Best brake pads........ Carbon Metallic???||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||09-17-2001||01:15 PM||||I'm looking for a high quality/best brake pad replacement for my 84.  I hear that carbon metallic pads are what I should look for but no one around here stocks them.  I also don't know any brands or such I should be looking for.  I'm looking for some information or perhaps opinions on what you guys use and have noticed to be the best.<P>The main use of the pads is for street and autocross.  I was told I need a pad with temp. range of about 100-800 (which is what I've found most to be)  <P>My reasoning here is to obtain a great brake upgrade without having to change calipers and rotors and such.  Also, I will be getting a set of the stainless steel brake lines from the FieroStore.  I heard of some products from the fierostore which aren't the best.  I need to know what expieriences people have been having with these.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||09-17-2001||03:14 PM||||the ss brake lines are a very good buy.  Actually costs slightly less then 4 replacement rubber hoses.  They're made by Earl's, a company that does a lot of stuff with braided ss hoses.  I have a set on my GT and my coupe.  I've never heard of anyone having a bad experience with these.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jncomutt||09-17-2001||06:14 PM||||Thanks alot standard.  I gave fierostore a call but it seems as though they are backordered and I'm going to have to wait for em.  (hope this doesn't turn out like the eibach spring expierience)  Do you know about the Carbomet brake pads they sell?  I'm really in need of some pads, and don't wanna go cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tomcat06085||09-17-2001||06:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jncomutt:<BR><B>I'm looking for a high quality/best brake pad replacement for my 84.  I hear that carbon metallic pads are what I should look for but no one around here stocks them.  I also don't know any brands or such I should be looking for.  I'm looking for some information or perhaps opinions on what you guys use and have noticed to be the best.<P>The main use of the pads is for street and autocross.  I was told I need a pad with temp. range of about 100-800 (which is what I've found most to be)  <P>My reasoning here is to obtain a great brake upgrade without having to change calipers and rotors and such.  Also, I will be getting a set of the stainless steel brake lines from the FieroStore.  I heard of some products from the fierostore which aren't the best.  I need to know what expieriences people have been having with these.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hey Jncomutt...I'm looking to have a Fiero ready for autocrossing by  spring. I'm in Unionville, CT and haven't raced legally for over thirty years, but my son is Pontiac fanatic and he's gonna set me up with a Fiero with handcontrols. So when you see the guy in the wheelchair getting strapped in the Fiero, that'll be me. I did it for seven years in the sixties and seventies with a Mini S and an MG Midget...lotta fun. Don't lock up your wheels...sliding and spinning means you're taking more time. I'm looking for an 88 with V6 and automatic transmission, so if you see one locally ( Connecticut ) let me know.<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Help me go fast!||||reg||14
Z||000004||2birds||09-17-2001||08:25 PM||||The stainless hoses I got from the Fiero Store are from Goodridge.  $80 for 4 was cheaper than the OEM versions from any parts store I checked, so it was a no-brainer.  I've had them on for 6 years.  The copper banjo bolt washers in some caliper rebuild kits are a bit too big, though, so you may need to take the banjo bolt to find and exact fit set (all 8 washers)  12 mm inside diameter, Raybestos p/n G24951.<BR>Carbomet pads were unimpressive, to me.  The brake dust was darker, and they were very noisy.  I now have Bendix semi-metallics, from Discount Auto (&lt;$20 an axle), and I love them.  Raybestos priced themselves out of my wallet. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||09-18-2001||10:23 PM||||I am very happy with <A HREF="http://www.carbotecheng.com" TARGET=_blank>Carbotech</A> Super Street pads on the front of my '85 SE.  They're <B>not</B> cheap, but they work quite well for me.  I think Carbotech also makes a serious autocross compound.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||09-19-2001||03:49 PM||||I had Fiero Store CarboMet pads on my first Formula. <I>Very</I> hard pedal until they get hot. When they were new, it was a almost scary to try to stop quickly, especially when it was cold outside. As they broke in, they grabbed a little better. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DreXteR||09-19-2001||05:01 PM||||Raydar - So the carbomet pads are bad?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||09-19-2001||11:27 PM||||I think what he's saying is that the pads need to be hot to work well.||||reg||1
